\section{Composite Structural Manifold}

Define the composite structural manifold:
\[
\mathcal{S}_{\text{TSSE–AITFE}} =
\mathcal{S}_{\text{T}}
\times
\mathcal{S}_{\text{RPK}}
\times
\mathcal{S}_{\text{FK}}
\times
\mathcal{S}_{\text{AITFE}},
\]
where:
\begin{itemize}
  \item $\mathcal{S}_{\text{T}}$ is the temporal symmetry manifold.
  \item $\mathcal{S}_{\text{RPK}}$ is the relativistic propulsion manifold.
  \item $\mathcal{S}_{\text{FK}}$ is the fusion gain manifold.
  \item $\mathcal{S}_{\text{AITFE}}$ is the AITFE structural manifold.
\end{itemize}

\begin{definition}[TSSE–AITFE Structural Manifold]
The TSSE–AITFE structural manifold is the direct product of all subsystem manifolds:
\[
\mathcal{S}_{\text{TSSE–AITFE}} = \prod_{i=1}^4 \mathcal{S}_i.
\]
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Composite Manifold Closure]
If each $\mathcal{S}_i$ is closed under its subsystem evolution, then:
\[
\mathcal{U}(t)\mathcal{S}_{\text{TSSE–AITFE}} = \mathcal{S}_{\text{TSSE–AITFE}}.
\]
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Since $\mathcal{U}(t)$ acts independently on each subsystem manifold and each
manifold is closed, the direct product is closed.
\end{proof}

This lemma ensures global structural stability of the integrated engine.

